        "knowledge_point": "Identifying the Graph of a Strict Inequality Given in Standard Form",
        "problem": "Sketch the solutions to the inequality $x+2y < 4$ in the $xy$-plane.",
        "worked_solution": "First, we convert the inequality into slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2y & <4 \\\\ 2y & <-x+4 \\\\ y & <-\\frac{1}{2}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed as usual. We start by drawing the corresponding straight line \n\n$$\ny=-\\dfrac{1}{2}x+2.\n$$\n\nSince the inequality involves a 'strictly less than' sign, the line $y=-\\dfrac{1}{2}x+2$ itself is *not* part of the solution. Therefore, we draw a dashed line.\n\nThen, we must decide whether to shade above or below the line. The inequality \n\n$$\ny < -\\dfrac{1}{2}x+2\n$$\n\nrefers to the $y$-values that are *less than* those on the line $y = -\\dfrac{1}{2}x+2,$ so we shade *below* the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/1450257c533c670d3ce0d803aa62b75c2ffe44159e26a3daf5dd3c04026a6fe4.png)",
